WEBVTT

00:00.150 --> 00:01.320
Instructor: So next up, we're gonna talk

00:01.320 --> 00:03.750
about one of my favorite commands in git,

00:03.750 --> 00:05.700
something called git reflog.

00:05.700 --> 00:07.620
It's not one that's used all that often

00:07.620 --> 00:08.940
but when it is used,

00:08.940 --> 00:10.050
at least when I use it,

00:10.050 --> 00:12.810
it's when I've screwed something up terribly

00:12.810 --> 00:13.950
and I've lost a commit,

00:13.950 --> 00:16.050
or I've rebased when I shouldn't have rebased,

00:16.050 --> 00:20.160
or I want to undo something that seems undoable.

00:20.160 --> 00:22.470
Well, with the help of the git reflog command,

00:22.470 --> 00:24.060
often we can fix things.

00:24.060 --> 00:26.940
We can dig ourselves out of some pretty bad situations.

00:26.940 --> 00:29.670
So, I recommend you check this section out.

00:29.670 --> 00:31.710
I think it's definitely worth your time.

00:31.710 --> 00:32.880
With that said,

00:32.880 --> 00:34.170
if all things go well,

00:34.170 --> 00:35.940
you won't be using any of what I show you

00:35.940 --> 00:37.710
very frequently at all.

00:37.710 --> 00:39.780
So, I'm not gonna say anything is critical.

00:39.780 --> 00:41.010
This is a sort of concept

00:41.010 --> 00:44.040
where you might just Google in a time of need.

00:44.040 --> 00:46.230
You might be desperate, "How do I fix this?"

00:46.230 --> 00:49.050
You know, "How do I retrieve this commit that I've lost?"

00:49.050 --> 00:51.630
And if you read some Stack Overflow post,

00:51.630 --> 00:53.970
it will tell you the exact git reflog command

00:53.970 --> 00:55.230
that you should use,

00:55.230 --> 00:57.330
so it's not something you need to commit to memory.

00:57.330 --> 01:02.160
Anyway, I'm gonna say everything in here is nice to have.

01:02.160 --> 01:03.810
Meh, I'll put it in important.

01:03.810 --> 01:05.970
It's between important and nice to have.

01:05.970 --> 01:09.450
Rescuing loss commits with reflog, undoing rebases.

01:09.450 --> 01:12.090
The one thing that I'll say really is not that critical,

01:12.090 --> 01:15.510
well, nothing's critical, but that's not as important,

01:15.510 --> 01:18.540
there's a video on time-based reflog qualifiers.

01:18.540 --> 01:20.730
You'll know it when you see it.

01:20.730 --> 01:22.770
It's not as important as this stuff.

01:22.770 --> 01:23.730
But anyway, remember,

01:23.730 --> 01:25.800
you won't be using this very frequently.

01:25.800 --> 01:26.880
If all goes well

01:26.880 --> 01:29.490
and you don't make a huge number of mistakes,

01:29.490 --> 01:31.680
you won't need to use this command very much.

01:31.680 --> 01:34.323
But when you do need it, it really is a lifesaver.
